ssary  shenanigans  considerably you want to comment on that  yeah i i would agree as i said i do have  some colleagues  especially those who know r and are  learning python for the first time it  can be really nice to not  introduce yet another editor it can be  challenging when you're trying to learn  a new language to also be learning  a new tool and so i think if you're  someone who knows r  that barrier of entry is low enough now  that you can get started  using python right within the rstudio  ide then if you want to graduate to  another editor  that's fine but at least you're not  learning both those things at once  and i kind of echo that as well to  educators that are out there you know  someone asked  what language should i teach well  i think that the key is to teach what  you're going to be comfortable teaching  to make sure that your students have a  really effective data science experience  from day one  you know that they're not stuck fighting  their tools  uh before they're able to create their  first plot whether that's a matte plot  lib or a gg plot two  you wanna give them that gratifying  moment early on  and it's my belief that a lot of folks  at our studio have done a ton of awesome  work  to ensure that our studio id isn't going  to present that hurdle and isn't going  to fight students  if you are a teacher interested in using  r and python also do a quick shout out  to our studio cloud  which can reduce that hurdle even  further by allowing folks to  to start writing code without installing  anything on day one  and that's a good segue to a question i  wanted to answer  about our studio cloud which is we had a  question is on whether our studio server  is running on premises or in the cloud  or what  our studio server pro typically um  most of our customers will install our  studio server pro themselves but where  they install it varies considerably  it could be on-prem and often is or it  could be  in a virtual private cloud on any of the  